<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bambuk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bambuk is a gold-bearing region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and Falémé rivers in present-day Senegal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. Its gold fields were a major source of the gold traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. They supplied the wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, and attracted the interest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bambuk.jpg" alt="Bambuk" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gabriel Moss · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Mali_Empire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bambouk is a traditional name for the territory that takes in the Bambouk Mountains on its eastern edge, the valley of the Faleme River and the hilly country east of it. It was a district of French Sudan until 1895, when the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> was moved to the Faleme and the western part fell within Senegal. The name is still used, but no administrative area bears it, and most of the region lies in the Kéniéba Cercle. It is home chiefly to the Malinké people, who speak a distinctive dialect of Maninkakan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Martin Meredith, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> nomads to run a packhorse route across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> between Lixus and the goldfields of Bambuk from roughly the 6th to the 2nd century BCE. Arab geographers counted Bambouk among the goldfields they called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Wangara</a>. Levtzion names it as one of three principal goldfields, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bure">Bure</a> on the Upper Niger and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> fields, and notes that wasteful methods reduced its output until, in the eleventh or twelfth century, traders opened the new fields of Bure.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> conquered Bambuk in the 13th century, the Diakhanke founded Diakhaba and served the Malinke chiefs as Muslim clerics, spreading from there to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bondu">Bondu</a>, Kedougou and Futa Djallon. The Portuguese reached Bambouk around 1550 but did not survive there. The French built Fort Saint Pierre on the Falémé in 1714 and two trading posts in 1724, abandoned in 1732 and 1759, and another post that lasted from 1824 to 1841.</p><p>The country was thinly settled by largely autonomous villages, and its mountains made it a refuge, but the villages seldom united and fell prey to Khasso and Bundu in the 18th and 19th centuries. The French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Louis Faidherbe</a> controlled the mines after 1860, yet output stayed low and engineers often died of disease. Some gold is still mined on the Malian side.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bambouk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Along this route gold from the <b>Bambuk</b> and Bure fields of the upper Senegal and Niger went north to Mediterranean markets, and control of it paid for the movement's conquests for decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They occupied the zone between the goldfields of <b>Bambuk</b> and Bure to the south and the Saharan caravan routes leading north to Berber and eventually Arab markets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mali's economy rested on control of the gold-producing regions of <b>Bambuk</b> and Bure, together with the salt mines of Taghaza in the Sahara and the trans-Saharan trade networks that linked them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mande-speaking Muslim merchants, the Wangara or Dyula, carried gold from the fields of <b>Bambuk</b> and Bure, kola nuts from the forest and cotton cloth, and brought back salt cut by slaves at Taghaza in the desert and copper from Takedda; they met North African traders at Walata and…</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Kantora's rulers controlled the highest navigable point on the river, where caravans from the upper Niger and <b>Bambuk</b> brought gold, ivory and slaves to trade for salt, cloth and European goods.</blockquote><a href="/state/kantora">Kantora</a></li><li><blockquote>Lying near the <b>Bambuk</b> goldfields and on the routes from the upper Senegal to the Gambia, it profited from caravans of gold, slaves, cloth and salt, and it warred repeatedly with Kaarta, Khasso and Gajaaga.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bundu">Kingdom of Bundu</a></li></ul></section>
